+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to production data
+
+`users.email` is required to be `NOT NULL` and unique case-insensitively, while
+the migration is required to leave every customer row untouched. Those two
+requirements are mutually exclusive for the current production snapshot
+(`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, 2026-10-01): the existing data already violates
+the constraint.
+
+## Violating rows in the snapshot
+
+Checked with `lower(email)` after loading `001_users.sql` + the snapshot:
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NULL` | id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) |
+| Duplicate under `lower(email)` = `ana.silva@example.com` | id 1 (`ana.silva@example.com`), id 4 (`Ana.Silva@example.com`), id 23 (`ana.silva@example.com`) |
+
+A unique index (and the `NOT NULL` copy) is rejected by SQLite for exactly
+these rows. There is no schema-only way to create the constraint while the
+rows remain as they are.
+
+## Why it is not worked around
+
+* The rows cannot be fixed by the migration: deduplicating ids 1/4/23 or
+  filling in / removing the NULL at id 17 means updating or deleting customer
+  data, which is explicitly out of scope (Support owns it).
+* Nor is the constraint silently skipped on non-conforming data (conditional
+  index, partial index, trigger-only enforcement): that would report success
+  while production remains unconstrained, and the snapshot test would pass for
+  the wrong reason.
+* Therefore `002_unique_email.sql` is written as the real constraint and fails
+  loudly and atomically on the snapshot. It does not mutate the database when
+  it fails: the copy/rebuild runs inside `BEGIN IMMEDIATE ... COMMIT`, the
+  `NOT NULL` check fails before the old table is dropped, and nothing is
+  committed.
+
+## Status
+
+* Fresh DB: the migration installs `email TEXT NOT NULL` plus a unique index on
+  `lower(email)`. Lower/upper-case duplicates and NULLs are rejected with
+  `sqlite3.IntegrityError`. `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+* Production snapshot: `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` cannot pass, because
+  applying the migration raises rather than succeeding. The guarantee it
+  encodes (rows unchanged) still holds — verified separately: the rows and the
+  `users` table are unchanged after the failed apply.
+
+## What Support needs to do before this can ship
+
+1. Decide the canonical address for the `ana.silva@example.com` group (ids 1,
+   4, 23) and give the other rows a distinct address, or merge/remove them.
+2. Fill in, or delete, id 17's NULL email.
+
+Suggested checks to confirm readiness (read-only):
+
+```sql
+SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;
+SELECT lower(email), group_concat(id) FROM users
+GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING count(*) > 1;
+```
+
+Both must return no rows; after that, `002_unique_email.sql` applies as-is.
